+# BLOCKED: `test_sla` timing target is physically unreachable
+
+## What is blocked
+
+`test_wordfreq.WordFreqTests.test_sla` requires `top_words()` to process the
+4,083,137-character (600,000-word) reference corpus in **under 1 ms**:
+
+```python
+self.assertLess(elapsed, 0.001)
+```
+
+The correctness half of the test (`got == want`) now passes. The timing
+assertion cannot pass for any implementation of this function in pure
+CPython, and no workaround was applied.
+
+## Why it is impossible
+
+The SLA leaves a budget of **1.67 ns per input word** (1 ms / 600,000), which
+is less than the cost of a single Python bytecode instruction. Measured on this
+machine (CPython 3.12.5, aarch64 macOS, best of 5 runs):
+
+| Operation (on the test corpus)              | Time      |
+| ------------------------------------------- | --------- |
+| `len(text)`                                 | 0.000 ms  |
+| `text.lower()` — one C-level pass, no tokenizing | **1.560 ms** |
+| `text.count(' ')` — scan only, no tokenizing | **4.808 ms** |
+| `text.split()` — cheapest tokenization into Python strings | **14.424 ms** |
+| `text.lower().split()`                      | 16.643 ms |
+| `Counter(text.lower().split())`             | 58.832 ms |
+| `re.findall(r"[a-z']+", text.lower())`      | 57.630 ms |
+| `sorted(Counter(...))[:10]` (full function) | 101.981 ms |
+
+The decisive point is that **the cheapest possible tokenization, `str.split()`,
+already takes 14.4 ms — ~15x over the 1 ms budget** — and counting the tokens
+is additional (58.8 ms with `Counter`). This holds even if `lower()` is
+avoided entirely (e.g. case-folding only the 5000 distinct tokens, or using
+`re.IGNORECASE`, which is slower still at 85 ms): `lower()` is not where the
+budget goes. A correct result requires materializing 600k Python string
+objects and grouping them, and at 1 ms that allows 1.67 ns per word — less than
+the cost of one bytecode instruction, and below the ~24 ns CPython needs just
+to allocate one `str`.
+
+Scanning 4.08 MB is not the bottleneck by itself (either `lower()` at 1.56 ms
+or `count(' ')` at 4.8 ms shows a single C pass is already at/over budget), but
+there is no stdlib C primitive that returns word counts without creating the
+token objects. The full implementation is ~100x over.
+
+`numpy` is installed, but it does not change the conclusion: encode + scan +
+boundary detection is already several ms, and grouping 600k variable-length
+tokens and producing Python `str` keys must still happen. No C extension or
+Cython is available in this environment, and even a hand-written fused C/Rust
+routine would need to tokenize and count 600k tokens within 1.67 ns each.
+
+## What was done instead
+
+- Rewrote `top_words()` to use `collections.Counter` (C-accelerated) instead of
+  the quadratic `seen.index()` scan. Same observable behaviour, verified
+  against the old implementation on 2,000 randomized inputs (case, apostrophes,
+  punctuation, empty strings, `n` values).
+- The corpus result is byte-for-byte correct.
+- Runtime dropped from **~6,363 ms to ~100 ms (~64x faster)** — the function is
+  no longer "way too slow" in practice, but it remains ~100x from the SLA.
+- `test_basic`, `test_case_and_apostrophes`, and `test_ties_alphabetical` pass.
+  `test_sla` fails only on `assertLess(elapsed, 0.001)` (0.101 s), not on the
+  correctness assertion.
+
+## What would unblock this
+
+Either:
+
+1. Raise the SLA to a feasible budget (roughly **150 ms** per 4 MB corpus is
+   realistic in pure Python), or
+2. Fund a compiled extension and accept a separate build/dependency, with the
+   caveat that even then 1 ms is unlikely to be achievable.
+
+Deliberately not done: faking `time.perf_counter`, precomputing/caching the
+result (the corpus is generated and passed once per test), returning a lazy
+object, or weakening/editing the test. Those would hide the problem rather than
+fix it.
